      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently reviewing a commercial lease agreement for a retail client in Singapore. Could you explain the standard implications of a \"force majeure\" clause if the tenant is facing a sudden, prolonged period of business interruption that isn't directly caused by a physical act of God, but rather by regulatory changes? I need to understand how this typically impacts the obligation to pay rent.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore, a standard force majeure clause typically covers unforeseen events such as natural disasters or other acts beyond the control of the parties. However, regulatory changes that disrupt business operations may not always fall under this clause unless specifically included. Generally, if a force majeure clause does not mention regulatory changes, the tenant would still be expected to pay rent even during business interruptions caused by such changes. It's crucial to re-evaluate the existing force majeure clause or negotiate an amendment to ensure clarity on this point.",
