    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. If the clause is silent on regulatory shifts, the risk falls heavily on the tenant. Looking at the wording of typical Singaporean commercial leases, do you see any specific phrasing regarding 'unforeseeability' or 'governmental intervention' that might allow for a more flexible interpretation during a sudden crisis?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. A force majeure clause that specifically mentions 'unforeseeability' or articulates a catch-all phrase like 'any subsequent event beyond the parties' control' may offer a broader interpretation during crises. If a commercial lease in Singapore has wording that includes 'any event or occurrence which the occurrence of which, either at any stage of commencement or during the term of the agreement, neither party shall be able to prevent through exercise of the highest degree of care, skill, diligence and prudence', this could potentially cover regulatory changes, as they would typically be less within the party's control to anticipate or prevent. However, as with all legal documents, the exact wording of the clause and its legal interpretation would ultimately depend on the judiciary's reading and the specific circumstances of the case at hand.",
      "source": "target",
